## 5. Configuration vs Coding ⭐

This is the most important design decision in Salesforce development.

### Configuration (Clicks / No-Code)

Using Salesforce's built-in tools — **no Apex code written**.

| Tool | What it does |
|------|-------------|
| **App Builder** | Drag-and-drop to create and customise pages |
| **Flow Builder** | Automate business processes (send email, create record, etc.) |
| **Object Manager** | Create custom objects, fields, relationships, validation rules |
| **Permission Sets** | Control who can see and do what |

**Examples — when to use Configuration:**
1. A new "Region" field needs to be added to the Account object → use **Object Manager** (clicks)
2. When an Opportunity is marked Closed Won, automatically send a congratulations email → use **Flow Builder** (clicks)

### Coding (Apex + LWC)

Writing actual code for logic that configuration tools cannot handle cleanly.

| Language | Used for |
|---------|---------|
| **Apex** | Backend business logic — triggers, complex calculations, integrations |
| **LWC** (Lightning Web Components) | Custom frontend UI using JavaScript |
| **REST / SOAP APIs** | Connecting Salesforce to external systems |

**Examples — when to use Code:**
1. When a deal closes, calculate a tiered commission that pulls data from 5 different objects and an external tax API → use **Apex** (too complex for Flow)
2. Build a custom interactive dashboard with animated charts not possible with standard components → use **LWC**

### Decision Rule

```
Can configuration solve it cleanly and maintainably?
    YES  →  Use Configuration  (faster, no deployment, easier to maintain)
    NO   →  Use Code (Apex / LWC)
```

> Always default to **configuration first**. Code is for things clicks genuinely cannot do.

