## 📝 Evaluation Questions — Answers

**1. What is an App in Salesforce?**

An App is a grouped collection of tabs, objects, and settings organised for a specific
business function. For example, the Sales App brings together Leads, Accounts, Contacts,
and Opportunities into one workspace for the sales team.

**2. What is an Object?**

An Object is a database table that stores a specific type of data. Standard objects like
Account and Contact come built-in. Custom objects can be created for business-specific
data — for example, a `Patient__c` object in a hospital system.

**3. Difference between App and Object?**

An Object stores data (like a table in a database). An App is a UI container that groups
multiple Objects together via Tabs into one workspace. An App does not store data — it
organises how users navigate to and interact with Objects.

**4. What is multi-tenant architecture?**

Multiple customers share the same Salesforce servers and codebase, but their data is
fully isolated from each other. Salesforce handles all infrastructure, upgrades, and
scaling — customers never manage servers. This makes it cost-effective and ensures
everyone always has the latest version automatically.

**5. When should we use configuration instead of code?**

Always try configuration first. If a business requirement can be solved cleanly using
Flow Builder, Object Manager, validation rules, or App Builder — use those. Code
(Apex/LWC) is only justified when the logic is too complex for clicks, requires external
system integrations, or needs performance that configuration tools cannot provide.

**6. How does Salesforce allow developers to extend functionality?**

Salesforce provides Apex (a Java-like language) for backend custom business logic,
LWC (Lightning Web Components) for building custom UI using JavaScript, and REST/SOAP
APIs for integrating with external systems. All of this runs on top of the platform layer —
developers never manage the underlying infrastructure.

